{
  "name": "Staircases",
  "_about": "STAIRS THAT FLY: each flight spans from landing to landing on a sloped soffit parallel to its own pitch (the rampant vault a stone stair is built on), and the landings are slabs of the same depth. A `staircase` is written as a WALK -- a flight, the landing it arrives on, and the flights that leave that landing `ahead`, `left`, `right` or `back` beside the one that came, each with a landing and flights of its own -- so the imperial stair, the dog-leg, the bifurcated T and the grand stairs of a baroque palace are all the same few lines. Where a flight meets a landing the two are one piece of stone, welded, with no face drawn between them. A `spiral-stair` is the same flight wound round a newel, its tread measured on the walking line, left- or right-handed, with or without its post.",
